From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SR2 Launcher <sr2-launcher@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] fex-patches: a code invalidation that re-enters the code
 buffer list walk

build/fex-patches/0002-fexcore-code-buffer-list-walk-re-entry.patch, built
into xtajit64.dll (and xtajit.dll) by build/fex-arm64ec/build.sh through
build/fex-patches/apply.sh, after Madeira's 0001.

FEXCore's InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange holds CodeBufferListLock while it
walks the code buffers. Releasing a buffer's shared_ptr there destroys it
when its owning thread let go meanwhile (a thread that exited during the
invalidation); ~CodeBuffer frees the JIT memory, Wine's ARM64EC ntdll
reports the free to NotifyMemoryFree, and the invalidation that asks for
comes back to InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange on the same thread, which
locks CodeBufferListLock a second time and waits on itself for good, the
code invalidation lock held. Slime Rancher 2 with MelonLoader's .NET 6:
the game thread 00e8 with two frames of InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked
on its stack, the .NET finalizer and the tiered-compilation worker in
"[fexlock] STUCK read-wait", 29 minutes without progress, right after

  [inval-nested] #1 invalidate 0x336c2d000: this thread already holds
  the code invalidation lock

The thread holding CodeBufferListLock is recorded; a call on that thread
while it holds the lock logs "[codebuf-nested]" and skips the walk. What
it names is FEX's own memory, never guest code.

Second part (build 8 showed the nested call then standing on the lookup
cache lock the outer InvalidateRange holds): InvalidateIntervalInternal
skips the whole invalidation when this thread already holds the code
invalidation lock ("[inval-nested-skip]"): it names FEX's own memory.

Third part (build 9 parked the game thread on the code buffer list lock
itself): the list lock is held only to take batches of references into
stack arrays and to append within capacity; invalidation, reference
release and the list's growth happen without it.
---
 ...xcore-code-buffer-list-walk-re-entry.patch | 259 ++++++++++++++++++
 build/fex-patches/README.md                   |   1 +
 2 files changed, 260 insertions(+)
 create mode 100644 build/fex-patches/0002-fexcore-code-buffer-list-walk-re-entry.patch

diff --git a/build/fex-patches/0002-fexcore-code-buffer-list-walk-re-entry.patch b/build/fex-patches/0002-fexcore-code-buffer-list-walk-re-entry.patch
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e33fc20
--- /dev/null
+++ b/build/fex-patches/0002-fexcore-code-buffer-list-walk-re-entry.patch
@@ -0,0 +1,259 @@
+FEXCore: a code invalidation that re-enters the code buffer list walk skips it
+
+ContextImpl::InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange holds CodeBufferListLock (a
+plain std::mutex) while it walks the list of code buffers. For each one it
+takes a shared_ptr, invalidates the lookup cache, and lets the shared_ptr go.
+When the buffer's owning thread let go of its own reference meanwhile (a
+thread that exited while this invalidation ran), that release is the last
+one and destroys the CodeBuffer. ~CodeBuffer frees the JIT memory with
+NtFreeVirtualMemory, Wine's ARM64EC ntdll reports the free to
+NotifyMemoryFree, and that asks for an invalidation of the freed range:
+InvalidateAlignedInterval -> LockCodeInvalidationUnlessHeld (held by this
+thread already: the nested path, "[inval-nested]") ->
+InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked -> InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange, which
+locks CodeBufferListLock a second time on the same thread and waits on
+itself for good. Every other thread then parks behind the code invalidation
+lock the thread also holds.
+
+Seen with Slime Rancher 2 (Unity IL2CPP, x64) once MelonLoader 0.7.3 starts
+its .NET 6 runtime (SR2 Launcher, iPhone 16 Pro Max, iOS 26.2.1): the game
+thread 00e8 in NtWaitForAlertByThreadId with two frames of
+InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked+0x48 on its stack, the .NET finalizer and
+the tiered-compilation worker in "[fexlock] STUCK read-wait", no further
+progress for 29 minutes. The log line right before the stall:
+
+  [inval-nested] #1 invalidate 0x336c2d000: this thread already holds the
+  code invalidation lock, taken at ra=30bcdad7c ..., here=30bcd9e38
+
+0x336c2d000 is in the JIT pool: the dying buffer's memory. The log's
+[waiters] line names the lock: tid=00e8 addr=0xb724f05b2 (the SRW lock
+behind the std::mutex, exclusive and with one waiter), the Mutex at
+0xb724f0108 being the code invalidation lock 0x4a8 bytes before it.
+
+The memory a nested call names is FEX's own (the dying buffer, the
+list's storage growing in OnCodeBufferAllocated, a lookup cache's entries
+released in GuestToHostMap::InvalidateRange), never guest code, so the
+lookup caches hold nothing for it. Two changes:
+
+1. InvalidationTracker::InvalidateIntervalInternal: when this thread
+   already holds the code invalidation lock (LockCodeInvalidationUnlessHeld
+   returns false), the call comes from FEX's own work under that hold, and
+   the invalidation is skipped with "[inval-nested-skip]". SR2 Launcher
+   build 8 had only change 2: the nested call then passed the code buffer
+   walk and stood on the next lock, the GuestToHostMap lock the outer
+   InvalidateRange holds, taken again in InvalidateThreadCachedCodeRange
+   -> LookupCache::InvalidateCacheRange ("[fexlock] STUCK write-wait on
+   mutex 0xb70d40230 ... owner tid=00e4", the same thread). The write-fault
+   path (HandleRWXAccessViolation) keeps its own handling.
+2. FEXCore: the thread that holds CodeBufferListLock is recorded
+   (CodeBufferListOwner, its thread id), and a call that arrives on that
+   thread while it holds the lock logs "[codebuf-nested]" and returns
+   without the walk. Kept as a second guard for callers that do not come
+   through InvalidateIntervalInternal.
+3. FEXCore: CodeBufferListLock is held only while nothing can block.
+   SR2 Launcher build 9 (changes 1 and 2) got past both earlier stops and
+   then parked the game thread on CodeBufferListLock itself, inside
+   InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange with the code invalidation lock held,
+   no nested frame on its stack, the .NET compiler thread and the new
+   thread-pool workers queued behind it. Under the old code the list lock
+   was held across InvalidateRange (a wait on each buffer's lookup cache
+   lock), across releasing buffer references (a CodeBuffer destructor
+   frees JIT memory) and across the list's growth in OnCodeBufferAllocated
+   (an allocation, which can wait on FEX's heap, Wine's virtual_mutex or
+   the memory notification it raises). Now:
+   - InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange takes batches of up to 32 references
+     out of the list into arrays on its stack, moving dead entries out
+     too, under the lock (no allocation, free or wait); it invalidates and
+     releases each batch after the lock is dropped. The index stays valid
+     between batches: every caller holds CodeInvalidationMutex
+     exclusively, so no other walk runs, and appends only go to the end.
+     A buffer appended mid-walk is new and empty, and nothing is compiled
+     into it before CodeInvalidationMutex is released.
+   - OnCodeBufferAllocated appends under the lock only within capacity;
+     to grow, it reserves larger storage without the lock, moves the
+     entries over under it, and frees the old storage after it.
+
+   launcher/tests/codebuf_lock runs these two functions, copied from the
+   patched Core.cpp, under the waits seen on the phone (allocations and
+   frees that ask for the code invalidation lock, buffers dying in the
+   walk). Build 9's versions hung in 10 of 30 two-second runs; these
+   finish every run, also under ThreadSanitizer.
+
+Applies on willfaust/FEX 1dc6ed4 (Madeira 0.2.0's FEX pin) after
+0001-windows-ios-smc-traps-per-16k-host-page.patch. SR2 Launcher's patch;
+Madeira-only, not for FEX-Emu.
+
+diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h
+index 3b3c7ac..15eca6a 100644
+--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h
++++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Context/Context.h
+@@ -513,6 +513,9 @@ private:
+   std::atomic<uint64_t> MonoBackpatcherBlock;
+ 
+   std::mutex CodeBufferListLock;
++  // SR2 Launcher: the thread holding CodeBufferListLock (FHU::Syscalls::gettid()), 0 while it is free.
++  // InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange reads it to recognise its own re-entry; see there.
++  std::atomic<int32_t> CodeBufferListOwner {0};
+   fextl::vector<std::weak_ptr<CPU::CodeBuffer>> CodeBufferList;
+ };
+ } // namespace FEXCore::Context
+diff --git a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
+index 8884ec0..44c9606 100644
+--- a/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
++++ b/FEXCore/Source/Interface/Core/Core.cpp
+@@ -667,10 +667,40 @@ void ContextImpl::OnCodeBufferAllocated(const fextl::shared_ptr<CPU::CodeBuffer>
+     Symbols.RegisterJITSpace(Buffer->Ptr, Buffer->AllocatedSize);
+   }
+ 
+-  {
+-    std::scoped_lock lk {CodeBufferListLock};
+-    CodeBufferList.emplace_back(Buffer);
++  /* SR2 Launcher: CodeBufferListLock is held only while nothing can block, so a thread waiting on it
++   * (InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange, under the code invalidation lock) never waits on a thread that is
++   * itself waiting. Growing the list allocates, and an allocation can block (FEX's heap, Wine's
++   * virtual_mutex, the memory notification it raises, which may want the code invalidation lock), so the
++   * larger storage is made here without the lock, filled under it by moves (no allocation), and the old
++   * storage is freed after the lock is dropped. */
++  const int32_t Self = FHU::Syscalls::gettid();
++  fextl::vector<std::weak_ptr<CPU::CodeBuffer>> Grown;
++  for (;;) {
++    size_t Needed = 0;
++    {
++      std::scoped_lock lk {CodeBufferListLock};
++      if (CodeBufferList.size() < CodeBufferList.capacity()) {
++        CodeBufferListOwner.store(Self, std::memory_order_relaxed);
++        CodeBufferList.emplace_back(Buffer); // within capacity: no allocation
++        CodeBufferListOwner.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
++        return;
++      }
++      if (Grown.capacity() > CodeBufferList.size()) {
++        CodeBufferListOwner.store(Self, std::memory_order_relaxed);
++        for (auto& Entry : CodeBufferList) {
++          Grown.emplace_back(std::move(Entry)); // within Grown's capacity: no allocation
++        }
++        Grown.emplace_back(Buffer);
++        CodeBufferList.swap(Grown); // Grown now holds the old storage and moved-from entries
++        CodeBufferListOwner.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
++        break;
++      }
++      Needed = std::max<size_t>(CodeBufferList.size() * 2, 64);
++    }
++    Grown.clear();
++    Grown.reserve(Needed);
+   }
++  // Grown (the old storage) is released here, without the lock.
+ }
+ 
+ void ContextImpl::ClearCodeCache(FEXCore::Core::InternalThreadState* Thread, bool NewCodeBuffer) {
+@@ -2358,14 +2388,68 @@ void ContextImpl::InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange(uint64_t Start, uint64_t Length
+   FEXCORE_PROFILE_SCOPED("InvalidateCodeBuffersCodeRange");
+ 
+   LOGMAN_THROW_A_FMT(CodeInvalidationMutex.try_lock() == false, "CodeInvalidationMutex needs to be unique_locked here");
+-  std::scoped_lock lk {CodeBufferListLock};
+-  auto it = CodeBufferList.begin();
+-  while (it != CodeBufferList.end()) {
+-    if (auto Strong = it->lock()) {
+-      Strong->LookupCache->InvalidateRange(Start, Length);
+-      it++;
+-    } else {
+-      it = CodeBufferList.erase(it);
++  /* SR2 Launcher (Madeira on iOS): CodeBufferListLock is not recursive, and this walk can come back
++   * to itself on the same thread. Releasing `Strong` below destroys a CodeBuffer whose owning thread
++   * let go of it meanwhile (a thread that exited while this invalidation ran). Its destructor frees
++   * the JIT memory, Wine's NtFreeVirtualMemory reports that free to NotifyMemoryFree, and the
++   * invalidation it asks for arrives here with the list lock still held by this thread: it waited on
++   * itself for good, and every other thread parked behind the code invalidation lock it also holds
++   * (Slime Rancher 2 with MelonLoader's .NET 6: the game thread, the .NET finalizer and the
++   * tiered-compilation worker, for 29 minutes). The memory a nested call names is FEX's own (the
++   * dying buffer, or the list's storage growing in OnCodeBufferAllocated), never guest code, so
++   * there is nothing in the lookup caches to invalidate for it: skip the walk. */
++  const int32_t Self = FHU::Syscalls::gettid();
++  if (CodeBufferListOwner.load(std::memory_order_relaxed) == Self) {
++    static std::atomic<uint32_t> NestedCount {0};
++    const auto N = NestedCount.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed) + 1;
++    if (N <= 16 || (N % 4096) == 0) {
++      LogMan::Msg::EFmt("[codebuf-nested] #{} invalidate {:#x}+{:#x}: this thread is walking the code buffer list, skipped", N,
++                        Start, Length);
++    }
++    return;
++  }
++  /* SR2 Launcher: CodeBufferListLock is held only to take a batch of references out of the list and
++   * to drop dead entries: fixed-size arrays on the stack, so nothing allocates, frees or waits under it.
++   * The lookup caches are invalidated, and the references released (which can destroy a CodeBuffer and
++   * free its JIT memory), after the lock is dropped, one batch at a time. Before, both happened under
++   * the lock: a destroyed buffer's free came back here on this thread, and any wait inside
++   * InvalidateRange held every other thread out of the list (Slime Rancher 2 with MelonLoader's .NET 6,
++   * build 9: the game thread parked on this lock for good, with the code invalidation lock held).
++   *
++   * Index stays valid across batches: every caller holds CodeInvalidationMutex exclusively, so no
++   * other walk runs, and OnCodeBufferAllocated only appends. A buffer appended during the walk is new
++   * and empty; nothing can be compiled into it before CodeInvalidationMutex is released, because
++   * compilation holds it shared. */
++  constexpr size_t BatchSize = 32;
++  std::shared_ptr<CPU::CodeBuffer> Live[BatchSize];
++  std::weak_ptr<CPU::CodeBuffer> Dead[BatchSize];
++  size_t Index = 0;
++  bool Done = false;
++  while (!Done) {
++    size_t LiveCount = 0, DeadCount = 0;
++    {
++      std::scoped_lock lk {CodeBufferListLock};
++      CodeBufferListOwner.store(Self, std::memory_order_relaxed);
++      while (Index < CodeBufferList.size() && LiveCount < BatchSize && DeadCount < BatchSize) {
++        if (auto Strong = CodeBufferList[Index].lock()) {
++          Live[LiveCount++] = std::move(Strong);
++          Index++;
++        } else {
++          Dead[DeadCount++] = std::move(CodeBufferList[Index]); // the entry left behind is empty
++          CodeBufferList.erase(CodeBufferList.begin() + Index);   // moves empty-or-live weak_ptrs: no frees
++        }
++      }
++      Done = Index >= CodeBufferList.size();
++      CodeBufferListOwner.store(0, std::memory_order_relaxed);
++    }
++    for (size_t i = 0; i < LiveCount; i++) {
++      Live[i]->LookupCache->InvalidateRange(Start, Length);
++    }
++    for (size_t i = 0; i < LiveCount; i++) {
++      Live[i].reset(); // may destroy a CodeBuffer: outside the list lock
++    }
++    for (size_t i = 0; i < DeadCount; i++) {
++      Dead[i].reset();
+     }
+   }
+ }
+diff --git a/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp b/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp
+index 073d558..6e2a442 100644
+--- a/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp
++++ b/Source/Windows/Common/InvalidationTracker.cpp
+@@ -925,10 +925,27 @@ bool InvalidationTracker::LockCodeInvalidationUnlessHeld(const char* Path, uint6
+ 
+ void InvalidationTracker::InvalidateIntervalInternal(uint64_t Address, uint64_t Size) {
+   const bool Locked = LockCodeInvalidationUnlessHeld("invalidate", Address);
+-  InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked(Address, Size);
+-  if (Locked) {
+-    CTX.GetCodeInvalidationMutex().unlock();
++  if (!Locked) {
++    /* SR2 Launcher: this thread already holds the code invalidation lock, so no guest code runs on
++     * it and this call comes from FEX's own work under that hold: memory it frees, allocates or
++     * reprotects (a dead CodeBuffer's JIT memory destroyed by the code buffer walk, a lookup cache's
++     * entries released in GuestToHostMap::InvalidateRange, the list's storage). Wine reports every
++     * such operation back as a range to invalidate. Running that invalidation here re-enters FEX's
++     * own non-recursive locks on this thread: CodeBufferListLock, then the GuestToHostMap lock the
++     * outer invalidation holds (Slime Rancher 2 with MelonLoader's .NET 6 stood on each in turn, for
++     * good). None of that memory holds guest code, so there is nothing to invalidate: skip it. The
++     * write-fault path keeps its own handling. */
++    static std::atomic<uint32_t> Skipped {0};
++    const auto N = Skipped.fetch_add(1, std::memory_order_relaxed) + 1;
++    if (N <= 16 || (N % 4096) == 0) {
++      LogMan::Msg::EFmt("[inval-nested-skip] #{} invalidate {:#x}+{:#x}: under this thread's own code invalidation hold, "
++                        "FEX's own memory, skipped",
++                        N, Address, Size);
++    }
++    return;
+   }
++  InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked(Address, Size);
++  CTX.GetCodeInvalidationMutex().unlock();
+ }
+ 
+ void InvalidationTracker::InvalidateIntervalInternalLocked(uint64_t Address, uint64_t Size) {
